\section{Define the Owner Before Choosing the Allocator}

The IRDL account of MLIR describes operations with SSA operands
and results, blocks of operations, and regions of connected
blocks. A nested operation can own a region; an operand's
reference to a value is not ownership. The distinction is
useful well beyond MLIR \cite{lattner2021mlir}. Assume here
that a function owns its mutable IR, an instruction can have
several results, and deleting a block is responsible for
detaching its instructions and edges. A value identifier can
be an instruction handle paired with a small result index,
provided rewriting the instruction cannot silently reinterpret
that index. Block parameters require their own value variant
in this design. A simple implementation gives all values their
own handles to avoid identity changes on result-list edits.

An arena is first a lifetime decision: all objects owned by it
ultimately expire together. A bump allocator serves immutable
or append-only objects particularly well: it can allocate by
advancing a cursor and reclaim storage in bulk. In a long-lived
mutable IR full of edits, that alone leaves every removed
payload occupying space. A slot arena can reclaim during
individual erasure while retaining arrays and free lists sized
for the peak live count. Decide separately whether destruction
of payloads is required; releasing bump pages does not execute
destructors for objects with owned memory or other resources.
The compiler's arenas are distinct from language-level heap
management at runtime; see \Cref{ch:ext-418} for the latter
integration boundary.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Allocation strategy & Strength & Important limit \\
\hline
Bump arena & Cheap allocation and bulk retirement & No slot reuse by erasure \\
Slot arena & Cheap handle validation and reuse & Holes can hurt iteration \\
Slots plus dense payloads & Fast iteration with deletion & Back-pointer needs repair \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Make Reuse Observable to a Stale Reference}

Consider work queued for instruction at slot 7. A rewrite
erases that instruction and creates an unrelated one. If the
queued item contains only slot 7, it will now modify the wrong
instruction. A generational handle contains arena provenance,
slot index, and generation. Valid lookup requires that the
right arena owns a live slot of the expected generation. In
\Cref{fig:visual-423}, the handle for the new instruction can
be dereferenced; the handle copied by the old worklist cannot.
Use distinct handle types for blocks, values, and instructions
so accidentally putting one in an operand becomes a type error
instead of a surprising successful lookup.

