\section{Move Payloads Without Renaming the Survivors}

\begin{verbatim}
find_slot(arena, h):
    if h.owner != arena.owner: return Missing
    if h.slot >= arena.slots.length: return Missing
    s := arena.slots[h.slot]
    if not s.live or s.generation != h.generation:
        return Missing
    return s

erase(arena, h):
    s := require_live_slot(arena, h)
    require detached_from_graph(arena, h)
    row := s.dense_row
    last := arena.dense.length - 1
    removed := arena.dense[row]
    if row != last:
        moved := arena.dense[last]
        arena.dense[row] := moved
        arena.slots[moved.slot].dense_row := row
    arena.dense.truncate_after_move(last)
    s.live := false
    if s.generation == MAX_GENERATION:
        s.retired := true
    else:
        s.generation += 1
        arena.free.push(h.slot)
    destroy(removed)
\end{verbatim}

This is move pseudocode: the removed payload is extracted,
and only the removed payload is destroyed. In a real
implementation use the container's swap-remove operation or a
carefully implemented equivalent, so that a noncopyable payload
is not destroyed twice. Allocation takes a free slot or appends a
fresh slot, appends a dense row storing the slot number, and
publishes the row index before marking the slot live. Erasure
repairs the relocated occupant's slot link. It is essential to
verify both directions: each live slot finds the row that
claims it, and each dense row has exactly one live slot.

The generation does not validate cached pointers to a row.
A growth of the dense array or a swap-removal can move a
payload while leaving its handle valid. Expose short-lived
borrows only while the arena is not mutating; retain handles
instead of those borrows across calls to rewriting passes.
This sketch uses exclusive access during mutation. A checked
lookup followed by unsynchronized use is not a concurrency
protocol: another thread can erase or move the payload
between the check and the load.

\section{Choose Lifetimes for Scratch Storage and Side Tables}

Stable handles let many passes share a common IR; they do not
imply that all analysis uses a slot-indexed array. If a function
loses eighty percent of its instructions after inlining and
simplification, a table sized by the largest allocated slot
is mostly holes. Build dense numbers for analyses that visit
all live nodes of one graph revision. Record the mapping in
both directions for diagnostics and invalidate it when nodes
are added or erased. A generation-stamped scratch side table
is a useful alternative for sparse updates; reset the entry
whenever the recorded handle generation differs. Both options
need a separate check for semantic freshness after an operand
change that leaves the node alive.

Do not wrap a generation silently. With an eight-bit generation,
repeatedly rewriting the same slot can recreate the original
bit pattern quickly. Retire exhausted slots as in the
pseudocode, or impose an owner epoch that invalidates every
externally held handle before rebuilding. Arena provenance must
also not be reused while a prior arena's handles can survive.
Persisting slot bits in an object file is not a strategy for
stable exported IDs; assign an artifact-local namespace during
serialization instead.

\section{Test the Contract Independently of Optimization}

Keep two handles alive, erase the first, and assert the
second still reaches its original payload after the slot's
row was moved. Allocate again and check that the new handle
can point to the freed slot while the stale handle fails. Use
a tiny generation width in a test build to exercise retirement,
and try the same slot and generation in a distinct arena.
At the pass boundary, scan every operand and edge for invalid
handles; stale optional worklist entries can be skipped, but
a stale authoritative graph reference is an invariant violation.

Finally measure peak slots, dense rows, and wasted payload
capacity separately. A bump arena can be faster for a short
phase even if it leaves all obsolete records allocated until
phase exit. The decision should follow edit frequency and
lifetime rather than a desire to make every allocation in
the compiler use one policy.

\section{Do Not Use the Allocator as a Graph Commit Protocol}

Suppose cloning a loop reserves two new blocks before cloning
their instructions. An instruction in the first block may
already need the ID of a target in the second. Reserving valid
handles early is useful, but a lookup succeeding must not
imply that a verifier can traverse the half-built function.
One strategy stages the clone in an edit transaction under
exclusive access: allocate skeletons, construct a mapping from
original to clone, then install the mapped edges and operands.
Validate shape and types before attaching the cloned blocks
to their final owners. If the clone is abandoned, erase the
private staging records rather than restoring a previously
retired generation.

Mutating the same arena in a transaction avoids remapping
the newly minted handles at commit. A separately owned
staging arena gives stronger isolation, but its handles cannot
cross unchanged into the function arena. Either way, avoid
storing a generation-stamped handle for an unfinished object
in a long-lived worklist before commit. Otherwise a stale
queued item can represent either a failed construction or a
deleted valid instruction, making it hard to distinguish a
broken mutation policy from a stale but harmless hint.
